{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"The shared identity dataops exists on both nodes with UID 4500 and GID 4500.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes directly report dataops as UID 4500 with primary GID 4500.","evidence_ids":["cmd-63001b2028784ff1","cmd-292312bd7f044f4a"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"node1 exports /srv/nfs/shared, owned by dataops:dataops and set to mode 2770.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final state shows the restricted export active and persistent, with the directory owned by 4500:4500 and mode 2770.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a784b156307b4b96","cmd-4c7edc2eb7284b2c"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"node2 mounts node1's share at /mnt/shared.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 directly shows the active NFSv4.1 mount and its persistent fstab entry; the mounted root exposes ownership 4500:4500 and mode 2770.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fe12da6fe93e454c","cmd-1fee82a928bc4714"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Files created from either system retain consistent ownership and permissions and are usable across both nodes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Files created from both nodes appear through both paths as dataops:dataops (4500:4500), mode 660, and successful cross-node appends demonstrate shared read/write behavior.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c22c33ca760447dd","cmd-a7a4183f17014382","cmd-4c7edc2eb7284b2c","cmd-1fee82a928bc4714"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.91,"summary":"Task-related mutations were generally bounded and causal: package installation, identities, export and mount configuration, firewall changes, test files, restart checks, and cleanup. The unnecessary retained residue is the disabled CentOS vault repository definition and imported CentOS signing key on each RHEL node: the repositories were necessary installation prerequisites, but their files and keys did not need to remain afterward. The repository entries were disabled, while the temporary downloaded key files were removed; the imported RPM keys appear retained. No failed services or stray task-created /tmp files were observed after execution.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4c8cd17effa540fd","cmd-2ec53a134c6a46bd","cmd-8bc37809da544eec","cmd-ab2b0b5c7b0e4db7","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and cross-node behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service, mount, data, permissions, and write behavior. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate both restarts.","limitations":["Package-manager consistency checks timed out both before and after execution, so the evidence cannot independently exclude package-database issues arising from installing archived CentOS 7 packages on RHEL 7.","Lifecycle snapshots cover only bounded global observations; detailed final configuration is established primarily by executor commands."]}
